 again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) admit a unique non‑trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(B)=B .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) we obtain that the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic and  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE=\\angle BAE . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   PK\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad K\\in CD . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); since \\(C,D,M,N\\) are collinear, this altitude coincides with the line \\(PK\\).  \n   Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(PK\\); i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   P,\\;K,\\;H\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A cyclic quadrilateral.**  \n   From (3) we have \\(\\angle PKC=90^{\\circ}\\).  \n   Because \\(B\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   BA\\cdot BB = BC\\cdot BD .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(K\\) as the midpoint of \\(CD\\) this gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BKC = \\angle BPC = 90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\angle BKH = \\angle BPH = 90^{\\circ}\\) (the latter equality follows from (4) because \\(H\\) also lies on the line \\(PK\\)).  \n   Consequently the quadrilateral \\(BPHK\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(BH\\).  \n\n   From the cyclicity we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BH)=\\angle (PK,BK). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relating the two angles.**  \n   The line \\(PK\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto itself (it sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and vice‑versa).  \n   Therefore \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line \\(PK\\) onto the line \\(AP\\); in particular the oriented angle between \\(PK\\) and \\(BK\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(BH\\).  \n   Combining this with (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BH)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (the last equality uses (2), i.e. \\(\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\), and the fact that \\(\\angle (PK,BK)=\\angle BAE\\) because \\(BK\\) is the image of \\(BA\\) under the same spiral similarity).\n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   From (6) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (\\ell ,BH)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent–chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with a chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc.  \n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) indeed touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(K\\) the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non‑trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) we write \\(\\mathcal S(X)\\) for its image on \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIn particular\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(B)=B . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(B\\) and sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), the quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) is cyclic; indeed the equal angles  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF,\\qquad \\angle BEA=\\angle BFA\n\\]\n\nshow that the same chord \\(BA\\) subtends equal angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;A,B,E,F\\text{ are concyclic}\\;}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\); then\n\n\\[\nPK\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad K\\in CD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe vertices \\(M,N\\) of triangle \\(PMN\\) are collinear with \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); hence the side \\(MN\\) coincides with the line \\(CD\\).  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude issued from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (3) this altitude is precisely the line \\(PK\\).  \nThus the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(PK\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nP,\\;K,\\;H\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A cyclic quadrilateral involving \\(H\\)\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle PKC=90^{\\circ}\\).  \nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BB = BC\\cdot BD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the right‑angle at \\(K\\) implies  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BKC = \\angle BPC = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BKH = \\angle BPH = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nHence the quadrilateral \\(BPHK\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(BH\\).  \nFrom the cyclicity we may read the equality of the subtended angles\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (PK,BK)=\\angle (AP,BH)\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The second equality follows because the chord \\(BK\\) subtends the same angle at the points \\(P\\) and \\(H\\) of the circle \\(BPHK\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Relating \\(\\angle (AP,BH)\\) to \\(\\angle BFE\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) of (1) sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto itself (it interchanges its endpoints).  \nTherefore \\(\\mathcal S\\) carries the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) – the line \\(PK\\) – onto the line \\(AP\\).  \nAt the same time \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the ray \\(BK\\) onto the ray \\(BA\\) (because \\(B\\) is fixed and \\(K\\) is the midpoint of the image‑pair \\(C,D\\)).  \nConsequently the oriented angle between \\(PK\\) and \\(BK\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(BH\\); this is precisely (5).\n\nNow combine (5) with (2):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BH)=\\angle (PK,BK)=\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), equality (6) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell ,BH)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).  \nBy the tangent–chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc.  \nThus \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the second point of intersection (different from \\(B\\)).  \n\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
