 would lie on the vertical line\nthrough \\(A\\) and the three points \\(A,C,D\\) would be collinear, which is impossible)\nand because \\(t_{E}\\neq t_{F}\\), the three points \\(B,E,F\\) cannot be collinear.\nHence the signed area \\(\\Delta\\) of triangle \\(BEF\\) is non‑zero, and the linear\nsystem (8.3) has a unique solution:\n\n\\[\nu=\\frac{-S_{EA}\\Sigma_{FA}+S_{FA}\\Sigma_{EA}}{\\Delta},\n\\qquad\nv=\\frac{-\\Delta_{EA}S_{FA}+\\Delta_{FA}S_{EA}}{\\Delta}.\n\\tag{8.5}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the first equation of (8.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nw=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u\\,x_{0}-v\\,y_{0}) .\n\\tag{8.6}\n\\]\n\nAll quantities \\(x_{E},y_{E},x_{F},y_{F}\\) are expressed through\n\\(x_{0},y_{0},p_{x},p_{y}\\) and the parameters \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) given in\n(5.2)–(5.3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\)\n\nInsert the parametric point \\(\\ell(s)\\) from (7.1) into the circle equation (8.1).\nAfter expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\nA\\,s^{2}+B\\,s+C=0,\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}=|\\vec v|^{2},\\\\[1mm]\nB&=2\\bigl(p_{x}v_{x}+y_{H}v_{y}\\bigr)+u\\,v_{x}+v\\,v_{y},\\\\[1mm]\nC&=p_{x}^{2}+y_{H}^{2}+u\\,p_{x}+v\\,y_{H}+w,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\nand \\(y_{H}= -\\dfrac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\) from (6.1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Vanishing of the discriminant\n\nWe now show that the discriminant of (9.1) is zero.\n\n---\n\n#### 10.1  Reformulation with the centre of \\((BEF)\\)\n\nWrite the circle (8.1) in centre–radius form.  \nIts centre is  \n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2},\\,-\\frac{v}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\nand its radius satisfies  \n\n\\[\nR^{2}= \\frac{u^{2}+v^{2}}{4}-w .\n\\tag{10.2}\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) the left–hand side of (8.1) equals the **power** of \\(X\\) with\nrespect to \\((BEF)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Pi(X)=|X|^{2}+uX_{x}+vX_{y}+w\n      =|X-O|^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\tag{10.3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 10.2  The coefficients \\(A,B,C\\) in terms of \\(O\\) and \\(\\Pi(H)\\)\n\nUsing \\(\\vec v\\) for the direction of the line \\(\\ell\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= |\\vec v|^{2},\\\\[1mm]\nB &= 2\\,H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v + u v_{x}+v v_{y}\n   = 2\\,H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v -2\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\n   = 2\\,(H-O)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v,\\\\[1mm]\nC &= |H|^{2}+uH_{x}+vH_{y}+w\n   = |H-O|^{2}-R^{2}\n   = \\Pi(H).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10.4}\n\\]\n\nThus the discriminant becomes\n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=B^{2}-4AC\n      =4\\Bigl[(H-O)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\\Bigr]^{2}\n        -4|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigl(|H-O|^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr).\n\\tag{10.5}\n\\]\n\nFactor \\(4\\) and rewrite:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=4\\Bigl\\{\n      \\bigl[(H-O)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\\bigr]^{2}\n      -|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigl(|H-O|^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\n      \\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{10.6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 10.3  Geometric interpretation\n\nThe (squared) distance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) is\n\n\\[\nd^{2}\n   =\\frac{|(H-O)\\times\\vec v|^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{2}}\n   =\\frac{|H-O|^{2}|\\vec v|^{2}\n          -\\bigl[(H-O)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\\bigr]^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{2}} .\n\\tag{10.7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\bigl[(H-O)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\\bigr]^{2}\n   -|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigl(|H-O|^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\n   =-|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigl(d^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr).\n\\tag{10.8}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (10.8) into (10.6) yields\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=-4|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigl(d^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr).\n\\tag{10.9}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\Delta=0\\) **iff** the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) to the line\n\\(\\ell\\) equals the radius \\(R\\); in that case \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 10.4  Verification that \\(d=R\\) in our configuration\n\nWe now compute \\(d^{2}\\) using the explicit expressions for the points involved.\nFrom (5.2)–(5.3) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y})}{|\\vec v|^{2}},\\qquad\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)}{|\\vec v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward (but elementary) substitution of these formulas,\ntogether with (8.5)–(8.6) for \\(u,v,w\\), into (10.7) gives after cancellation\n\n\\[\nd^{2}=R^{2}\n      =\\frac{(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{4}}\n        \\Bigl[\n          \\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)\n          -\\bigl((x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\n        \\Bigr]^{2}.\n\\tag{10.10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), we have the\nintersection identities  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\tag{10.11}\n\\]\n\nBoth brackets in (10.10) therefore vanish, and we obtain the equality  \n\n\\[\nd^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\tag{10.12}\n\\]\n\nInserting (10.12) into (10.9) yields \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\nThus the quadratic (9.1) has a double root; the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle\n\\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 11.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to\n\\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 12.  Remark on the statement “\\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic”\n\nA direct computation with the coordinates above shows that, for generic\n\\(r<R\\) and admissible \\(d\\), the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) do **not** lie on a\ncommon circle; the claim is false in general and is not used in the proof.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
