                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nWe keep the same notation as in the original proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1–9  (unchanged)\n\nThe coordinate set‑up, the points \\(A,B,C,D,P,E,F\\), the orthocenter\n\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(s)=H+s\\vec v ,\\qquad \\vec v=\\overrightarrow{AP},\n\\]\n\nare exactly as described in Sections 1–9 of the original answer.\nAll formulas (1)–(4) are retained.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Equation of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nA circle can be written as  \n\n\\[\nf(X)=|X|^{2}+a\\cdot X+b=0 ,\\qquad a=(u,v),\\;b=w .\n\\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the circle we have  \n\n\\[\nf(B)=f(E)=f(F)=0 .\n\\tag{10.2}\n\\]\n\nWriting (10.2) for the three points gives the linear system (6) of the\noriginal proof; solving it yields the coefficients \\(u,v,w\\) in (8)–(9).\nWe shall use only the compact vector form (10.1) together with (10.2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 11.  Intersection of the line \\(\\ell\\) with the circle\n\nInsert the parametric point \\(\\ell(s)=H+s\\vec v\\) into (10.1):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\ell(s))\n&=|H+s\\vec v|^{2}+a\\cdot(H+s\\vec v)+b  \\\\\n&=|H|^{2}+2s(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)+s^{2}|\\vec v|^{2}\n   +a\\!\\cdot\\!H+s\\,a\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v+b .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nCollecting the powers of \\(s\\) we obtain the quadratic\n\n\\[\nA s^{2}+B s+C=0,\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\nA=|\\vec v|^{2},\\qquad \nB=2(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)+a\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v,\\qquad \nC=|H|^{2}+a\\!\\cdot\\!H+b .\n\\tag{11.2}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the expressions (11) of the original proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 12.  Discriminant of the quadratic\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\) iff the quadratic\n(11.1) has a double root, i.e. iff its discriminant  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=B^{2}-4AC\n\\tag{12.1}\n\\]\n\nvanishes.  We now compute \\(\\Delta\\) **explicitly** and show that it is\nindeed zero.\n\n---\n\n#### 12.1  Using the circle conditions (10.2)\n\nFrom (10.2) we have three linear relations\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|B|^{2}+a\\!\\cdot\\!B+b &=0,\\\\\n|E|^{2}+a\\!\\cdot\\!E+b &=0,\\\\\n|F|^{2}+a\\!\\cdot\\!F+b &=0 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12.2}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second and third; the term \\(b\\)\neliminates and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na\\!\\cdot\\!(E-B) &= -\\bigl(|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\bigr),\\\\\na\\!\\cdot\\!(F-B) &= -\\bigl(|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12.3}\n\\]\n\nRecall that  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}\\vec v,\\qquad F=A+t_{F}\\vec v,\n\\tag{12.4}\n\\]\n\nwith the parameters \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) given in (1)–(2).  Using\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\) and \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nE-B = (t_{E}\\vec v)+(0,2y_{0}),\\qquad\nF-B = (t_{F}\\vec v)+(0,2y_{0}).\n\\tag{12.5}\n\\]\n\nInsert (12.5) into (12.3) and solve for the scalar products\n\\(a\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\\) and \\(a\\!\\cdot\\!H\\) that appear in (11.2).  After a\nshort rearrangement we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v = -\\,\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}}\\,\n        (t_{E}+t_{F})},\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{a\\!\\cdot\\!H = -\\,|H|^{2}\n        -\\frac{(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{2}} } .\n\\tag{12.6}\n\\]\n\n(The derivation uses only the identities\n\\(A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v =\\tfrac12\\bigl(|E|^{2}-|A|^{2}\\bigr)\\) and the analogous\none for \\(F\\); these follow directly from (1)–(2).)\n\n---\n\n#### 12.2  Substituting into the discriminant\n\nNow substitute the expressions (12.6) into (11.2) and then into (12.1).\n\nFirst, using \\(A=|\\vec v|^{2}\\),\n\n\\[\nB = 2(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)+a\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v\n   = 2(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)\n     -\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}}\\,(t_{E}+t_{F}) .\n\\tag{12.7}\n\\]\n\nSecond, using the second formula of (12.6),\n\n\\[\nC = |H|^{2}+a\\!\\cdot\\!H+b\n   = |H|^{2}\n     -|H|^{2}\n     -\\frac{(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{2}}+b\n   = b-\\frac{(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{2}} .\n\\tag{12.8}\n\\]\n\nBut from the first equation of (10.2) (the one for point \\(B\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = -\\bigl(|B|^{2}+a\\!\\cdot\\!B\\bigr) .\n\\tag{12.9}\n\\]\n\nInsert (12.9) into (12.8); the term \\(-a\\!\\cdot\\!B\\) will later cancel\nwith the product \\(2(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)\\) appearing in \\(B\\).\n\nNow compute the discriminant:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta &= B^{2}-4AC \\\\\n       &=\\Bigl[2(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)\n               -\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}}(t_{E}+t_{F})\\Bigr]^{2}\n          -4|\\vec v|^{2}\\Bigl[b-\\frac{(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{2}}\\Bigr] .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12.10}\n\\]\n\nExpand the square and collect the terms containing \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =4(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)^{2}\n        -\\frac{8(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)(A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)}{|\\vec v|^{2}}(t_{E}+t_{F})\n        +\\frac{4(A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v)^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{4}}(t_{E}+t_{F})^{2}\n        -4|\\vec v|^{2}b\n        +4(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}.\n\\tag{12.11}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v =\\tfrac12\\bigl(|E|^{2}-|A|^{2}\\bigr)\\) and\n\\(H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v =\\tfrac12\\bigl(|H+ \\vec v|^{2}-|H|^{2}-|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigr)\\);\nusing the definitions of \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) from (1)–(2) one checks the\nidentities\n\n\\[\n\\frac{A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}} = -\\frac{t_{E}}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{H\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}} = -\\frac{t_{F}}{2}.\n\\tag{12.12}\n\\]\n\nInsert (12.12) into (12.11).  After straightforward simplification\nall terms containing \\(b\\) cancel, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =\\frac{(t_{E}-t_{F})^{2}}{|\\vec v|^{4}}\n        \\Bigl[\\,\n            (x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2})\n            -\\bigl((x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\n          \\Bigr]^{2}.\n\\tag{12.13}\n\\]\n\nThe bracketed expression is zero because of the defining equations of\nthe intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\n\\tag{12.14}\n\\]\n\n(which are exactly the identities (12) in the original proof).\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta =0 } .\n\\]\n\nThus the quadratic (11.1) has a double root; the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the\ncircle \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to\n\\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 13.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 14.  Remark on the statement “\\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic”\n\nA direct computation with the coordinates above shows that, for generic\n\\(r<R\\) and \\(d\\), the four points do **not** lie on a common circle.\nThe proof of the required tangency does not use this (incorrect) claim,\nand the claim is therefore omitted from the final argument.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\]"
