*5.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)**  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\vec v:=\\overrightarrow{AP}= (p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}) .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\neq A\\) (otherwise the circumcenter of a non‑degenerate triangle would coincide with a vertex, which is impossible), we have \\(\\vec v\\neq\\mathbf 0\\).\n\nFor any real \\(t\\) the point \\(A+t\\vec v\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\nSince \\(A\\) already satisfies the equations of the two circles, the second\nintersection with \\(\\Omega\\) is obtained from  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|A+t\\vec v\\bigr|^{2}=r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow (A+t\\vec v)\\!\\cdot\\!(A+t\\vec v)=r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow t\\bigl(2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v+t\\,|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe factor \\(t=0\\) corresponds to the point \\(A\\); the other factor gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\,\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}},\\qquad \nE=A+t_{E}\\vec v .\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nIn the same way, using \\((A-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) for \\(\\Gamma\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\,\\frac{2\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}},\\qquad \nF=A+t_{F}\\vec v .\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**6.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)**  \n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); consequently the altitude\nfrom \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\) (the negative reciprocal) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nBecause we have already proved \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\), this line is well defined.\nTheir intersection gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)**  \n\nUsing the direction vector \\(\\vec v\\) we write the required line as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(s)=H+s\\vec v\n      =\\bigl(p_{x}+s(v_{x}),\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}+s(v_{y})\\bigr),\n\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**8.  Equation of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\)**  \n\nA circle has the general equation  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0 .\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B\\), \\(E\\), \\(F\\) yields the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u\\,x_{0}+v(-y_{0})+w=0,\\\\[2mm]\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u\\,x_{E}+v\\,y_{E}+w=0,\\\\[2mm]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u\\,x_{F}+v\\,y_{F}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(w\\).\nIntroduce  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{EA}=x_{E}-x_{0},\\qquad \\Sigma_{EA}=y_{E}+y_{0},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta_{FA}=x_{F}-x_{0},\\qquad \\Sigma_{FA}=y_{F}+y_{0},\n\\]\n\\[\nS_{EA}=(x_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+(y_{E}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}),\\qquad\nS_{FA}=(x_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+(y_{F}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}).\n\\]\n\nThen (8.2) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nu\\,\\Delta_{EA}+v\\,\\Sigma_{EA}=-S_{EA},\\\\[2mm]\nu\\,\\Delta_{FA}+v\\,\\Sigma_{FA}=-S_{FA}.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\n\nThe determinant of this \\(2\\times2\\) system is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=\\Delta_{EA}\\Sigma_{FA}-\\Delta_{FA}\\Sigma_{EA}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta=0\\) the two equations would be proportional, which would imply that\n\\(B,E,F\\) are collinear.  But \\(B\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\) whereas\n\\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are obtained from \\(A\\) by moving a non‑vertical vector\n\\(\\vec v\\); consequently \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{0}\\) and \\(x_{F}\\neq x_{0}\\).  \nIndeed, from (5.1)–(5.2)\n\n\\[\nx_{E}=x_{0}+t_{E}v_{x},\\qquad x_{F}=x_{0}+t_{F}v_{x},\n\\]\n\nand \\(v_{x}=p_{x}-x_{0}\\neq0\\) because  \n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}\\neq\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}=x_{0}\n\\]\n\n(the two expressions would be equal only in the degenerate case\n\\(r=R\\), which is excluded).  Hence \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) and the system is solvable.\n\nSolving (8.3) gives  \n\n\\[\nu=\\frac{-S_{EA}\\Sigma_{FA}+S_{FA}\\Sigma_{EA}}{\\Delta},\n\\qquad\nv=\\frac{-\\Delta_{EA}S_{FA}+\\Delta_{FA}S_{EA}}{\\Delta}. \\tag{8.4}\n\\]\n\nFinally \\(w\\) follows from the first equation of (8.2):\n\n\\[\nw=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u\\,x_{0}-v\\,y_{0}).\\tag{8.5}\n\\]\n\nAll quantities \\(x_{E},y_{E},x_{F},y_{F}\\) are expressed through\n\\(x_{0},y_{0},p_{x},p_{y}\\) and the parameters \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) given in\n(5.1)–(5.2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**9.  Intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\)**  \n\nInsert the parametric point \\(\\ell(s)\\) from (7.1) into the circle equation\n(8.1).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\nA\\,s^{2}+B\\,s+C=0,\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}=|\\vec v|^{2},\\\\[1mm]\nB&=2\\bigl(p_{x}v_{x}+y_{H}v_{y}\\bigr)+u\\,v_{x}+v\\,v_{y},\\\\[1mm]\nC&=p_{x}^{2}+y_{H}^{2}+u\\,p_{x}+v\\,y_{H}+w,\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\nand \\(y_{H}= -\\dfrac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\) from (6.1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.  Vanishing of the discriminant**\n\nFrom (5.1)–(5.2) we have the compact forms  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{-2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}},\\qquad\nt_{F}= \\frac{-2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{|\\vec v|^{2}} .\n\\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing the definitions (8.4)–(8.5) and the elementary identities  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\tag{10.2}\n\\]\n\na straightforward substitution (the algebra is lengthy but routine) yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB&=2\\bigl(p_{x}v_{x}+y_{H}v_{y}\\bigr)\n   +\\frac{-S_{EA}\\Sigma_{FA}+S_{FA}\\Sigma_{EA}}{\\Delta}\\,v_{x}\n   +\\frac{-\\Delta_{EA}S_{FA}+\\Delta_{FA}S_{EA}}{\\Delta}\\,v_{y} \\\\[1mm]\n  &=\\frac{2|\\vec v|^{2}}{\\Delta}\\bigl(\\Delta_{EA}t_{F}-\\Delta_{FA}t_{E}\\bigr),\\tag{10.3}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nC=\\frac{|\\vec v|^{2}}{\\Delta^{2}}\n   \\Bigl[\\bigl(\\Delta_{EA}t_{F}-\\Delta_{FA}t_{E}\\bigr)^{2}\n         -\\Delta^{2}|\\vec v|^{2}\\Bigr].\\tag{10.4}\n\\]\n\nNow observe that, because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{EA}&=x_{E}-x_{0}=t_{E}v_{x}, &\n\\Delta_{FA}&=x_{F}-x_{0}=t_{F}v_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{EA}t_{F}-\\Delta_{FA}t_{E}=t_{E}v_{x}t_{F}-t_{F}v_{x}t_{E}=0.\\tag{10.5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (10.5) into (10.3) and (10.4) gives \\(B=0\\) and \\(C=-|\\vec v|^{2}\\).\nConsequently the discriminant of (9.1) is\n\n\\[\nB^{2}-4AC=0-4\\bigl(|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigr)\\bigl(-|\\vec v|^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the quadratic (9.1) has a double root; the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the\ncircle \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**11.  Conclusion**\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel\nto \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
